    {
      "type": "breakdown",
      "heading": "Sentence breakdown",
      "sentence": {
        "line": {
          "gurmukhi": "ਮੇਰੇ ਦੋ ਭਰਾ ਹਨ।",
          "romanised": "Mere do bhra han.",
          "english": "I have two brothers."
        }
      },
      "glosses": [
        { "gurmukhi": "ਮੇਰੇ", "romanised": "mere", "meaning": "of me / my", "grammarNote": "oblique form of mera — changes with the noun" },
        { "gurmukhi": "ਦੋ", "romanised": "do", "meaning": "two", "grammarNote": "number sits before the noun, like English" },
        { "gurmukhi": "ਭਰਾ", "romanised": "bhra", "meaning": "brothers", "grammarNote": "plural noun" },
        { "gurmukhi": "ਹਨ", "romanised": "han", "meaning": "are", "grammarNote": "verb — always last" }
      ],
      "note": "Word order: \"of-me two brothers are.\" The number goes before the noun, and the verb still lands at the end."
    },
    {
      "type": "grammar",
      "heading": "How numbers behave",
      "body": "Punjabi numbers go directly before the noun: ਇੱਕ ਗਲਾਸ (one glass), ਦੋ ਭਰਾ (two brothers). There is no word for \"of\" between a number and a noun in counting — \"ik glass paani\" literally stacks one + glass + water.\n\nNotice that the verb \"to have\" is usually expressed with ਮੇਰੇ/ਮੇਰੀ ਕੋਲ (with me) or simply \"mere X han\" — \"of-me X are\" — rather than a direct verb like English \"have\"."
    },
    {
      "type": "whyThisWay",
      "heading": "Why is it said this way?",
      "body": "Gurmukhi has its own digit symbols, but in markets, buses, and texts you will see a mix: prices often in Arabic digits, formal documents in Gurmukhi digits. Learning to recognise ੧–੧੦ is useful, but the spoken words matter more at this stage.\n\nThe \"mere do bhra han\" pattern feels backwards at first, but it mirrors how many Punjabi possession sentences work — the things exist \"of you\" rather than you \"having\" them."
    },
    {
      "type": "conversation",
      "heading": "Counting at home",
      "conversation": {
        "title": "How many brothers?",
        "context": "Simran asks Gurnaam about his family.",
        "lines": [
          {
            "speaker": "Simran",
            "line": {
              "gurmukhi": "ਤੁਹਾਡੇ ਕਿੰਨੇ ਭਰਾ ਹਨ?",
              "romanised": "Tuhaade kinne bhra han?",
              "english": "How many brothers do you have?"
            }
          },
          {
            "speaker": "Gurnaam",
            "line": {
              "gurmukhi": "ਮੇਰੇ ਦੋ ਭਰਾ ਹਨ।",
              "romanised": "Mere do bhra han.",
              "english": "I have two brothers."
            }
          },
          {
            "speaker": "Simran",
            "line": {
              "gurmukhi": "ਅਤੇ ਭੈਣਾਂ?",
              "romanised": "Ate bhainaan?",
              "english": "And sisters?"
            }
          },
          {
            "speaker": "Gurnaam",
            "line": {
              "gurmukhi": "ਮੇਰੀ ਇੱਕ ਭੈਣ ਹੈ।",
              "romanised": "Meri ik bhain hai.",
              "english": "I have one sister. (meri — because bhain is feminine)"
            }
          }
        ]
      }
    },
